text:
Demonstration Versus Production Runs
The workflow scripts use short, single-chain settings to test integration and make iterative development feasible. A successful demonstration run shows that inputs align, native samplers execute, and outputs can be formatted. It does not establish convergence or robust inference.
Production analyses generally require:

substantially longer burn-in and retained sampling;
repeated chains or otherwise defensible convergence assessment;
trace, mixing, effective-sample-size, and posterior predictive diagnostics;
sensitivity analysis for prior settings and annotation regularization;
sensitivity to LD thresholds, LD windows, scaling, and marker selection;
reproducible seeds, software versions, and input provenance;
held-out validation or cross-fitting where data-derived priors are reused.

No universal chain length can be recommended from the current workflows. Required effort depends on marker count, sparsity, LD, prior complexity, annotation collinearity, and the inferential target.

Streaming and Blocking Behavior
The writer reads the selected BED markers into a packed representation, then works through pairs of marker blocks. For each eligible block pair it:

decodes standardized marker values into floating-point working buffers;
computes raw block cross-products with matrix multiplication;
converts each eligible cross-product to correlation (r_{ij});
applies distance and (r^2) retention rules; and
appends retained upper-triangle column indices and values to disk.

After each row, it writes the cumulative retained-entry count to row_ptr. Consequently, the final row pointer equals nnz.
Streaming avoids holding the final col_idx and values arrays in R or in one complete native in-memory result. It does not eliminate all memory or runtime costs: the packed selected genotype matrix, diagonal cross-products, decoded blocks, and block cross-products still require resources. The number of candidate block pairs can also become large when windows are wide or disabled.

Sparse LD as an Approximation
The sparse matrix replaces unretained off-diagonal relationships with zero. Approximation enters through:

(r^2) thresholding;
marker-index and physical-distance windows;
finite-sample LD estimation;
allele-frequency estimation and missing-genotype handling;
32-bit storage of retained correlation values; and
any difference between the sample or scaling used for LD and sufficient statistics.

Increasing r2_threshold or narrowing windows usually reduces nnz, runtime, and disk use, but can remove LD needed to represent the conditional marker likelihood adequately. Lower thresholds and wider windows preserve more LD at greater computational cost. There is no universally correct threshold; its adequacy depends on marker density, LD structure, sample size, phenotype architecture, and inferential target.

BED-Versus-CSR Comparison and Reproducibility
When both paths use the same BED source, markers, samples, phenotype, standardization, and priors, stblr_bed_marker() and stblr_csr() should usually show qualitatively comparable posterior patterns. Exact equality is not expected because:

the CSR path omits unretained LD;
BED and CSR samplers maintain residual information differently;
update scheduling and initialization can differ;
retained correlations use finite precision; and
MCMC introduces Monte Carlo variation.

A comparison should examine PIP rankings, posterior mean effects, aggregate variance summaries, and sensitivity to thresholds rather than equality of individual samples.
For reproducibility, retain:

immutable identifiers for BED inputs and selected samples;
marker IDs in exact CSR order;
cls, rows, allele frequencies, and positions;
sufficient-statistic dimensions and scaling choices;
all files belonging to ld_prefix;
the writer’s returned summary and metadata;
package version and platform information; and
model priors, MCMC settings, scheduling controls, and seeds.

The current workflow scripts use demonstration-scale settings. Production analyses require sensitivity checks for LD construction and sampler settings, longer chains, and convergence diagnostics.

Connection to Current sblr Interfaces
The public sblr() wrapper describes yy as a trait cross-product matrix, Xy as marker-trait cross-products, and XX as marker cross-product or LD matrices. It initializes trait variances from the diagonal of yy. However, the native contracts differ across its legacy algorithm choices: several older generated interfaces expose yy as a vector, whereas the CSR MTBLR kernel described below explicitly consumes a full matrix. Users and developers must verify the exact selected algorithm before assuming that off-diagonal SSY entries are used.
The generated mtblr_cpg_omp_csr() interface accepts:

wy, one marker vector per trait;
ww, marker diagonal cross-products per trait;
yy, a full (TT) trait cross-product matrix;
ld_prefix, identifying shared CSR LD;
marker-effect covariance B;
residual covariance E;
multi-trait sharing models and model probabilities; and
a sample-size vector n.

The current CSR kernel requires equal values in n and matching ww across traits because it assumes shared LD scaling. It does not accept Nmat directly. Therefore, Nmat currently serves as an analysis-level object used to construct, justify, and validate SSY; it is not automatically used by the native CSR likelihood to adjust each trait pair.
This distinction matters. A mathematically reconstructed SSY for partial overlap does not by itself remove the kernel’s shared-(X), shared-LD, and equal-(n) assumptions. More general partial-overlap likelihoods require additional implementation and validation.

Inspecting and Validating CSR Files With sparseLD_read_CSR()
Before fitting, sparseLD_read_CSR() can inspect the files at ld_prefix:
ld &lt;- sparseLD_read_CSR(ld_prefix, one_based = TRUE)

stopifnot(
  ld$nrow == length(cls),
  ld$ncol == length(cls),
  length(ld$row_ptr) == length(cls) + 1L
)
The reader returns the row pointers, column indices, values, dimensions, and metadata. one_based = TRUE makes returned column indices convenient for R inspection; one_based = FALSE exposes their zero-based disk convention. This option changes only the returned inspection object. The CSR samplers read the disk representation through package code and handle its index base.
Useful checks include confirming:

matrix dimensions equal the selected marker count;
row pointers have length (m+1);
column indices fall within the expected base and dimensions;
metadata records the intended r2_threshold, distance limits, and sample count;
the number of stored values agrees with the final row pointer.

The binary-file and metadata details are documented further in Technical sparse LD and CSR.

Comparing BED-Direct and CSR Posterior Outputs
When both representations are prepared from the same individual-level data, stblr_bed_marker() provides a useful comparison for stblr_csr(). Compare posterior quantities that share the same marker and trait order, such as posterior mean effects fit$bm and posterior inclusion probabilities fit$dm.
plot(fit_csr$bm[, 1], fit_bed$bm[, 1],
     xlab = "CSR posterior mean effect",
     ylab = "BED posterior mean effect")
The fits should agree qualitatively when markers, sample rows, phenotypes, scaling, allele frequencies, priors, and MCMC settings are matched. Strong signals and broad posterior patterns should be recognizable across paths. Exact equality is not expected because:

CSR LD is a thresholded and possibly distance-limited approximation;
BED and CSR samplers can use different update scheduling;
finite MCMC chains introduce Monte Carlo variation;
differences in initialization, residual adjustment, or prior settings change the posterior;
floating-point and storage precision differ across calculations.

A comparison is an integration diagnostic, not a proof that either fit has converged or that the sparse-LD approximation is adequate.

Runtime, Disk, Reproducibility, and Convergence Cautions
Both sufficient-statistic construction and sparse-LD construction can be runtime-heavy. block_size and nthreads affect construction performance, while r2_threshold and distance restrictions also affect the statistical LD approximation. Record all of them.
Disk-backed CSR preparation writes multiple files sharing ld_prefix. Treat them as one artifact: moving, replacing, or mixing files from different runs can invalidate the prefix. Use distinct prefixes for different marker sets, sample subsets, scaling choices, and LD thresholds.
For reproducibility, retain:

marker IDs in model order;
BED paths or immutable genotype-data identifiers;
cls, rows, af, and phenotype preprocessing details;
sufficient-statistic dimensions and trait names;
the complete CSR prefix and its metadata;
sampler priors, scheduling controls, seeds, and MCMC settings.

The short chains in workflow scripts demonstrate object handoffs. Real analyses require longer chains and appropriate convergence and posterior predictive checks. Compact output guidance is covered in Workflows and outputs.

Why Model Traits Jointly
Biologically related traits often share part of their genetic architecture. A marker with a weak effect on one trait may be supported by stronger evidence from another trait, and a joint model can estimate genetic and residual covariances rather than treating every trait as independent.
The main reasons to use an MT-BLR model are:

to borrow information across correlated traits;
to represent markers that affect one, several, or no traits;
to estimate cross-trait marker-effect, genetic, and residual covariance;
to improve inference for weak signals when the assumed sharing structure is appropriate.

This is different from fitting a single-trait model independently to every column of a phenotype or summary-statistics matrix. Parallel single-trait fits can be useful and computationally convenient, but they do not jointly update marker effects or estimate cross-trait covariance during sampling.

Multi-Trait Regression Model
For (t) traits measured on a common set of (n) individuals, the individual-level model is
[ = + , ]
where:

() is the (n t) phenotype matrix;
() is the (n m) genotype matrix;
() is the (m t) marker-effect matrix;
() is the (n t) residual matrix.

The (i)th row of (),
[ _i =
\[\begin{bmatrix}
b_{i1} & \cdots & b_{it}
\end{bmatrix}\]
^{T}, ]
is the effect vector for marker (i). A marker may have no effect, affect one trait, or affect several traits. Joint MT-BLR models use prior probabilities and covariance matrices to describe these possibilities.

Outputs and Interpretation
Multi-trait fits may return the following objects, depending on the selected sampler:

bm: posterior mean marker effects, with markers in rows and traits in columns;
dm: posterior inclusion summaries or PIPs by marker and trait;
d or o: current inclusion or sharing-model states, when returned;
vbs, vgs, and ves: sampled effect, genetic, and residual variance summaries;
covb, covg, and cove: posterior effect, genetic, and residual covariance summaries;
rb, rg, and re: corresponding correlation summaries, when returned;
pi or pim: posterior inclusion or sharing-model probability summaries.

Useful reporting separates trait-specific and cross-trait evidence:

summarize PIPs and posterior mean effects by trait;
list top markers separately for each trait;
identify markers with support across several traits;
report covariance and correlation estimates with uncertainty;
compare posterior sharing probabilities with their prior values.

Large marker-by-trait matrices should usually be summarized rather than printed. Posterior support is conditional on the model, priors, LD, cross-trait information, and MCMC settings. A high multi-trait PIP or shared pattern identifies a candidate under those assumptions; it does not establish causal direction.

Annotation-Informed Multi-Trait Directions
Annotations can, in principle, modify the prior probability of each trait-sharing pattern, the scale of active effects, or the covariance among traits. This creates several possible MT-BLR extensions:

marker-specific probabilities for trait-specific and shared patterns;
annotation-dependent active-effect variance or covariance;
group-specific sharing priors;
annotation-dependent mixture components.

These extensions require careful regularization because overlapping annotations, many sharing patterns, and covariance estimation can create a large parameter space. In the current package, annotation simulations and annotation-informed single-trait CSR wrappers provide the main implemented foundation. They should not be described as a polished annotation-aware joint MTBLR workflow.

When to Use Multi-Trait Models
Consider a multi-trait model when:

traits are biologically or statistically related;
shared genetic architecture is scientifically meaningful;
trait scaling and phenotype definitions are compatible;
sample overlap is known or defensibly modeled;
the LD reference and marker ordering are compatible across traits;
there is enough information to estimate cross-trait covariance.

Prefer separate single-trait analyses, or use them as a diagnostic comparison, when trait definitions are incompatible, sample overlap is unknown, LD references differ materially, or cross-trait borrowing is not scientifically justified.

Practical Cautions and Diagnostics
Multi-trait models introduce more assumptions than single-trait models. Important checks include:

verify trait order across Xy, SSY, sample sizes, covariance matrices, and output columns;
verify marker order and scaling across all trait summary statistics and LD;
check that SSY and covariance matrices are symmetric and numerically plausible;
record sample-overlap assumptions;
compare joint results with separate single-trait fits;
inspect covariance traces and sharing-model probabilities;
assess sensitivity to sharing priors and covariance priors;
use longer chains, repeated chains, and convergence diagnostics for production inference.

Weak or unstable covariance estimates can lead to misleading borrowing across traits. Short demonstration chains are useful for integration testing, not for final scientific conclusions.

Current Status in sblr
The package contains a conceptual and implementation foundation for MT-BLR:

sblr() is the documented high-level interface for several experimental summary-statistics algorithms and exposes sharing-model and covariance inputs;
native multi-trait implementations explore dense, Armadillo-based, OpenMP, and CSR computations;
the CSR multi-trait kernel supports cross-trait phenotype information and shared sparse LD under relatively restrictive sample-size and scaling assumptions;
mtsim() and mtsim_annotation() support development and model checking.

The most complete current workflow examples focus on BED and CSR single-trait models, including annotation-informed extensions. There is not yet an equally polished, general, end-to-end public workflow for joint MTBLR with arbitrary sample overlap. Algorithm-specific argument contracts and returned objects should be validated before relying on an experimental MTBLR path.
This status distinction matters: the package contains real multi-trait model and sampler components, but some interfaces and broader overlap or annotation extensions remain research-stage.

Regularization, Caps, Shrinkage, and Overfitting
Annotation-informed priors can become aggressive because probabilities and variance scales enter marker updates repeatedly. The following controls are part of the model, not merely numerical safeguards:

center annotation linear predictors around their marker average;
standardize continuous annotations so coefficient scales are comparable;
decide deliberately whether binary annotations should remain 0/1 or be centered and standardized;
shrink annotation coefficients toward zero;
bound marker inclusion probabilities away from zero and one;
cap variance multipliers to avoid extreme marker scales;
use pseudo-counts and shrinkage for small annotation sets;
inspect correlated annotation columns and unstable coefficient directions;
use cross-fitting or external estimates for formal empirical-Bayes inference.

The default bounds in current wrappers are intentionally broad and are not scientific defaults for every analysis. Sensitivity to bounds, prior scales, annotation preprocessing, and LD construction should be reported.

Interpretation and Causal Limits
Annotation-informed models estimate how annotation structure relates to prior architecture and posterior genomic evidence under the fitted model. They can identify annotations associated with higher posterior inclusion, different effect scales, or different mixture-component probabilities. They can support biological prioritization and generate hypotheses.
They do not prove that an annotation, gene, tissue, or regulatory mechanism causes a phenotype. Apparent enrichment can reflect:

LD between annotated and causal markers;
marker density and annotation size;
correlated or overlapping annotations;
phenotype architecture and model misspecification;
sparse-LD approximation and marker filtering;
winner’s curse or reuse of the same data to estimate and evaluate priors.

Genetic sharing across traits is also not causal direction. Colocalization, Mendelian randomization, functional experiments, and external replication ask different questions and require their own assumptions.
Row alignment is essential: a misordered annotation matrix produces a well-formed but biologically meaningless prior. Short demonstration chains are only execution examples. Real analyses need longer chains, convergence and proposal diagnostics, sensitivity analysis, posterior predictive checks, and clear documentation of annotation preprocessing and prior construction.

Representation-Specific Limitations and Validation Checks
Individual-level and summary-statistics representations have different failure modes.
Individual-level BED fitting

requires access to individual genotypes and aligned phenotypes;
reads genotype blocks repeatedly during fitting;
can calculate observed phenotype and residual cross-products when data are jointly available;
still requires correct cls, rows, allele frequencies, and scaling.

Summary statistics plus sparse LD

can avoid repeated access to individual-level genotypes after preparation;
replaces the full (X^X) matrix with an explicit LD approximation;
requires compatible wy, ww, yy, (n), and LD;
requires SSY and overlap information for full joint multi-trait residual covariance calculations;
cannot recover information omitted during summarization or sparsification.

At minimum, validate the following before fitting:

cls contains no missing marker matches and has the expected length.
Allele frequencies correspond to the selected marker columns.
bed_xtx_xty() and sparseLD_stream_CSR() use identical marker order, sample rows, and scaling choices.
CSR dimensions and indexing are consistent with the number of markers.
Annotation rows and marker/group vectors use the same marker order.
stats$wy, stats$ww, and posterior marker matrices have compatible dimensions.
Trait names, stats$yy, and phenotype columns are aligned.
Multi-trait summary analyses state how SSY and sample overlap were observed, assumed, or reconstructed.

Comparing posterior summaries from stblr_bed_marker() and stblr_csr() on the same prepared data is a useful integration check, but exact numerical agreement is not expected automatically. Sparse-LD approximation, scaling, sampler scheduling, finite MCMC error, and prior settings can all affect the comparison.

Sparse Mixture Priors and Posterior Outputs
Sparse Bayesian regression allows many markers to have exactly zero current effects while a subset belongs to active components. The inclusion indicator (d_{it}) records whether marker (i) is active for trait (t). Across MCMC samples:

dm[i, t] estimates the posterior inclusion probability;
bm[i, t] estimates the posterior mean marker effect;
b and d may retain current or sampled marker states;
vbs, vgs, and ves record variance-component traces;
covb, covg, and cove summarize effect, genetic, and residual covariance where available.

Annotation models change how prior mass or effect scale is distributed before and during these updates. They do not change the requirement that marker rows in statistics, LD, annotations, and posterior summaries remain aligned.
The full marker-update algebra and annotation-prior derivations are deferred to Technical summary statistics and Technical annotation priors.

Implemented Models and Research Extensions
The model/function map above describes current public interfaces. The archived technical drafts also contain deeper or proposed directions, including:

empirical-Bayes loops that use first-stage marker summaries to estimate annotation-level priors for later genomic fits;
direct annotation-aware MTBLR sharing-model priors and covariance models;
sparse annotation representations and sampler implementation sketches;
warm-started regional MTBLR workflows;
reconstruction of cross-trait SSY and overlap matrices for joint summary-statistics models.

These ideas should be preserved because they explain the intended model landscape, but they must be labeled as implemented, experimental, or proposed when moved into maintained notes. Public function availability alone also does not guarantee that every model path has an equally polished end-to-end workflow.

Annotation Matrix and Preprocessing
Let
[ A =
\[\begin{bmatrix}
A_{11} & \cdots & A_{1K}\\
\vdots & \ddots & \vdots\\
A_{m1} & \cdots & A_{mK}
\end{bmatrix}\]
]
be an (m K) marker-by-annotation matrix. Row (A_i) describes marker (i), and column (k) describes an annotation. Binary annotations may encode membership in functional sets; quantitative annotations may encode continuous scores. A marker may belong to several annotations, so the columns generally do not define a partition.
The marker-row contract is strict. Rows of (A) must correspond, in the same order, to:

the markers used to compute wy and ww;
rows and columns of the sparse-LD matrix;
marker names represented by cls and Glist$rsidsLD; and
rows of posterior marker summaries such as bm and dm.

When row names and marker names are both available, the annotation preparation helper can reorder rows by name. Names are not a substitute for checking the alignment: duplicated, incomplete, or stale names can still produce an incorrect analysis.

Intercepts, binary columns, and quantitative columns
The annotation-aware wrappers use .stblr_prepare_annotation_matrix() to validate and preprocess (A). Its relevant behavior is:

values must be numeric and finite;
an intercept is recognized when the first column is all ones;
add_intercept = TRUE prepends an intercept if one is not recognized;
with standardize = TRUE, nonbinary, non-intercept columns are centered and scaled;
binary columns are left unchanged unless center_binary = TRUE.

An intercept lets the model express a baseline prior independently of the annotation contrasts. Centering quantitative columns makes their coefficients more comparable and reduces confounding between the intercept and annotation effects. Leaving a binary indicator uncentered gives its coefficient the interpretation of a contrast against markers outside that annotation.
These choices are part of the model specification and should be reported. Automatic standardization is convenient, but users should retain the preprocessing metadata needed to apply the same transformation in sensitivity analyses or prediction data.

Interpretation and Causal Limits
Annotation coefficients, set enrichments, and marker-specific priors describe how external information interacts with the fitted prior and observed genetic evidence. They do not prove that an annotation or annotated marker is causal.
Apparent enrichment can reflect:

LD between annotated and causal markers;
differences in marker density and allele frequency;
correlated or overlapping annotation definitions;
phenotype architecture and sample size;
sparse-LD approximation and marker filtering; and
model, regularization, and prior-bound choices.

Top markers and enriched annotations should therefore be treated as model-dependent evidence. Comparisons against unannotated models and sensitivity analyses are central to interpretation.

Implementation Checklist
Before fitting an annotation-informed model:

verify marker-row alignment across annotations, sufficient statistics, sparse LD, and posterior outputs;
inspect annotation column prevalence, ranges, missingness, and correlation;
reconsider all-zero, all-one, extremely rare, or nearly duplicate columns;
record intercept, centering, standardization, and binary-column choices;
record inclusion-probability and variance-multiplier bounds;
decide whether priors are external, empirically constructed, jointly learned, or group based;
use cross-fitting or external data when empirical-Bayes reuse is a concern;
compare prior annotation summaries with posterior annotation summaries; and
report sensitivity to priors, annotation preprocessing, and LD settings.

Practical Validation Checklist
Before interpreting an SBayesRC-style fit:

verify marker alignment among (A), sufficient statistics, CSR LD, and posterior summaries;
record the gamma grid and distinguish the sblr default from the published grid;
record intercept, standardization, and binary-centering choices;
inspect annotation prevalence, scaling, overlap, and collinearity;
report sigmaSqAlpha_a, sigmaSqAlpha_b, intercept_flat, pi_floor, and alpha_update_every;
check component counts, PIPs, variance traces, and chain behavior;
distinguish annotation-derived prior probabilities from posterior component frequencies in comp_prob;
compare annotation-row and full marker-profile diagnostics;
assess sensitivity to gamma, sparse-LD thresholds, annotations, and hyperparameters; and
avoid causal claims from alpha coefficients or expected-gamma summaries.

How stblr_csr() Consumes the Statistics
stblr_csr() accepts:

stats$wy, one marker vector per trait;
stats$ww, one positive marker-diagonal vector per trait;
stats$yy, one phenotype sum of squares per trait;
(n), supplied explicitly or taken from stats$n;
ld_prefix, identifying the compatible disk-backed sparse LD; and
prior, variance-update, scheduling, and MCMC controls.

The wrapper uses yy/(n-1) to initialize phenotype-variance-related prior scales. The native sampler validates marker and trait dimensions, reconstructs retained off-diagonal cross-products from LD correlations and ww, initializes or rebuilds
[ r=wy-b, ]
and performs marker and variance updates from the resulting summary-statistic state.
For the current shared-LD single-trait-per-trait CSR path, ww and (n) must agree across traits. This reflects one common genotype sample and scaling scheme. Supplying trait-specific summary statistics from materially different samples requires a model and LD contract that explicitly supports those differences.

Exact Equivalence and Practical Differences
Suppose the following are all exact and compatible:

the same centered or otherwise identically coded (X);
the same phenotype (y);
complete (wy=X^y), (yy=y^y), and (XX=X^X);
the same prior, initialization, and variance model; and
an exact implementation of the same update schedule.

Then individual-level and summary-statistics marker updates can target the same model family because their likelihood calculations are algebraically equivalent.
Practical BED and CSR fits are not expected to be numerically identical:

sparse LD omits weak or distant off-diagonal entries;
stored correlations and reconstructed cross-products use finite precision;
BED and CSR implementations can maintain residuals differently;
update scheduling and marker order can differ;
prior and residual-adjustment settings may differ; and
MCMC output contains Monte Carlo variation.

Agreement should therefore be assessed through posterior patterns, predictions, variance summaries, and sensitivity analyses rather than equality of individual draws.

3.1 R implementation
estimate_vg_by_set &lt;- function(fit, sets, n) {
  b &lt;- fit$bm
  q &lt;- fit$wy - fit$r

  out &lt;- matrix(NA_real_, nrow = length(sets), ncol = ncol(b))
  rownames(out) &lt;- names(sets)
  colnames(out) &lt;- colnames(b)

  for (s in seq_along(sets)) {
    idx &lt;- sets[[s]]

    for (t in seq_len(ncol(b))) {
      out[s, t] &lt;- sum(b[idx, t] * q[idx, t]) / n
    }
  }

  out
}
A proportion of total genomic variance can be computed as
vg_set &lt;- estimate_vg_by_set(fitST, sets, n)
prop_of_vg &lt;- sweep(vg_set, 2, colMeans(fitST$vgs), "/")
If phenotypes are standardized to variance approximately one, the values themselves can also be interpreted as approximate proportions of phenotypic variance.

4.1 R implementation
estimate_pip_enrichment &lt;- function(dm, sets) {
  out &lt;- matrix(NA_real_, nrow = length(sets), ncol = ncol(dm))
  rownames(out) &lt;- names(sets)
  colnames(out) &lt;- colnames(dm)

  all_idx &lt;- seq_len(nrow(dm))

  for (s in seq_along(sets)) {
    idx &lt;- sets[[s]]
    bg &lt;- setdiff(all_idx, idx)

    for (t in seq_len(ncol(dm))) {
      mean_in &lt;- mean(dm[idx, t])
      mean_bg &lt;- mean(dm[bg, t])
      out[s, t] &lt;- mean_in / mean_bg
    }
  }

  out
}
This is not a variance decomposition. It measures whether posterior inclusion probability is enriched in a marker set.

5.1 R implementation
estimate_weighted_vg_by_set &lt;- function(fit, sets, n) {
  b &lt;- fit$bm
  dm &lt;- fit$dm
  q &lt;- fit$wy - fit$r

  out &lt;- matrix(NA_real_, nrow = length(sets), ncol = ncol(b))
  rownames(out) &lt;- names(sets)
  colnames(out) &lt;- colnames(b)

  for (s in seq_along(sets)) {
    idx &lt;- sets[[s]]

    for (t in seq_len(ncol(b))) {
      out[s, t] &lt;- sum(dm[idx, t] * b[idx, t] * q[idx, t]) / n
    }
  }

  out
}

6.3 R implementation: fractional response model for PIP
annotation_glm_dm &lt;- function(dm, annot, trait, eps = 1e-6) {
  p &lt;- pmin(pmax(dm[, trait], eps), 1 - eps)
  X &lt;- as.data.frame(annot)

  glm(p ~ ., data = X, family = quasibinomial())
}

8.1 R implementation
estimate_annotation_pi &lt;- function(dm, sets, alpha = 1) {
  nt &lt;- ncol(dm)
  K &lt;- length(sets)

  pi_ann &lt;- matrix(NA_real_, K, nt)
  rownames(pi_ann) &lt;- names(sets)
  colnames(pi_ann) &lt;- colnames(dm)

  for (k in seq_along(sets)) {
    idx &lt;- sets[[k]]

    for (t in seq_len(nt)) {
      pi_ann[k, t] &lt;- (alpha + sum(dm[idx, t])) / (2 * alpha + length(idx))
    }
  }

  pmin(pmax(pi_ann, 1e-8), 1 - 1e-8)
}
For marker-specific priors, assign each marker to an annotation or compute a marker-specific value from overlapping annotations.

13.1 R implementation
estimate_annotation_model_pi &lt;- function(dm, models, sets, alpha = 1) {
  M &lt;- nrow(dm)
  nt &lt;- ncol(dm)
  K &lt;- length(sets)
  nmodels &lt;- length(models)

  W &lt;- matrix(0, M, nmodels)

  for (j in seq_len(nmodels)) {
    model &lt;- models[[j]]
    p &lt;- rep(1, M)

    for (t in seq_len(nt)) {
      dmt &lt;- pmin(pmax(dm[, t], 1e-6), 1 - 1e-6)
      p &lt;- p * if (model[t] == 1) dmt else (1 - dmt)
    }

    W[, j] &lt;- p
  }

  pi_ann &lt;- matrix(NA_real_, K, nmodels)
  rownames(pi_ann) &lt;- names(sets)
  colnames(pi_ann) &lt;- sapply(models, paste, collapse = "_")

  for (k in seq_along(sets)) {
    idx &lt;- sets[[k]]
    counts &lt;- colSums(W[idx, , drop = FALSE]) + alpha
    pi_ann[k, ] &lt;- counts / sum(counts)
  }

  pi_ann
}
In the MTBLR marker update, a global model prior
loglik[k] = std::log(pi[k]) - 0.5 * logdet + 0.5 * quad;
can be replaced by an annotation-specific model prior:
loglik[k] = std::log(pi_annotation[ann_i][k]) - 0.5 * logdet + 0.5 * quad;
or a marker-specific prior:
loglik[k] = std::log(pi_marker(i, k)) - 0.5 * logdet + 0.5 * quad;

21.9 Sparse annotation matrix implementation
For large numbers of markers and annotations, the annotation matrix \(A\) should be stored sparsely. For each marker \(i\), store the list of active annotations:
struct AnnotationNeighbor {
  int k;
  float value;
};

using AnnotationRows = std::vector&lt;std::vector&lt;AnnotationNeighbor&gt;&gt;;
Then the linear predictor for marker \(i\) is:
double eta_i = intercept;
for (const auto& a : annot_rows[i]) {
  eta_i += static_cast&lt;double&gt;(a.value) * eta[a.k];
}
For STBLR:
const double pi_i = inv_logit(eta_pi_i);
const double vb_i = std::exp(eta_vb_i);
For MTBLR sharing models:
for (int model = 0; model &lt; nmodels; ++model) {
  log_pi_model[model] = base_log_pi[model];
  for (const auto& a : annot_rows[i]) {
    log_pi_model[model] += a.value * eta_model(a.k, model);
  }
}

// Then apply softmax to get pi_marker_model[i, model].
This avoids constructing dense marker-by-model or marker-by-trait prior matrices when \(m\) is large.
